{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 50 square units","reason":"Let O be the midpoint of AB, so OC=12. Since CO is a median, its centroid G satisfies OG=OC/3=4. The completed locus is therefore a circle of radius 4, with area 16π≈50.265, which rounds to 50.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The traced locus omits the images of A and B. The requested bounded area is interpreted using the circular completion of that locus.","The locus itself omits two points. As indicated by the task, the enclosed area is interpreted using its circular completion."],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-ratio-homothety","label":"Scale the circle using the centroid ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-ratio-homothety":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-coordinate-locus","label":"Find the centroid coordinates and circle equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-coordinate-locus":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h176"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h176"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-circle-scaling","label":"Scale the circle using the centroid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-circle-scaling":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","cnm_p192"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","cnm_p192"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: the intersection of the medians","reason":"The centroid gives each triangle one third of the total area. Conversely, equal areas on the two sides of each vertex-to-point line force that line to pass through the opposite side's midpoint.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p148","cnm_055","cnm_h013"],"id":"characterize-medians-by-area","label":"Recognize medians through equal areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"characterize-medians-by-area":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_113"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_055","cnm_h013"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: a straight line","reason":"If M is the fixed midpoint of AB, then G=M+(C-M)/3. A homothety of ratio 1/3 maps the line traced by C to a line traced by G.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Only positions of C producing a nondegenerate triangle are admissible. Excluding degenerate positions can remove points from the supporting straight-line locus."],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113"],"id":"centroid-dilation","label":"Scale from the fixed midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-dilation":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","cnm_113"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 24n","reason":"MP is the image of AB under a half-scale homothety centered at C, so Q is the midpoint of CN. Since ON=CN/3, OQ=ON/2. Also [CMN]=[ABC]/4 and [OMN]=[CMN]/3, giving [OMQ]=[ABC]/24.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinate-base-height","label":"Place AB horizontally","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-base-height":["cnm_084","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p148"],"id":"median-midsegment-area-ratios","label":"Use median, midsegment, and common-height ratios","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-midsegment-area-ratios":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. sqrt(17)","reason":"Take the centroid as origin and write A=(a,0), B=(0,b), C=(-a,-b). Then AC^2=4a^2+b^2=36 and BC^2=a^2+4b^2=49. Adding gives a^2+b^2=17, so AB=sqrt(17).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-right-triangles","label":"Use centroid right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-right-triangles":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174","cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-coordinate-axes","label":"Put the medians on coordinate axes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-coordinate-axes":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h087","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h087","cnm_h173"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_075"],"id":"1970_AMC_12_Q28_m1","label":"Use right triangles along the medians","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1970_AMC_12_Q28_m1":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174","cnm_113","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 64","reason":"At centroid G, BG=(2/3)·8=16/3 and CG=(2/3)·12=8. Therefore area BGC=(1/2)(16/3)(8)=64/3. Since this is one third of area ABC, the required area is 64.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_p148"],"id":"centroid-and-area","label":"Use the centroid and compare areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-and-area":["math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_113","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 50","reason":"Let O be the midpoint of AB. Since OC = 12 and OG = OC/3, the centroid traces a circle of radius 4, apart from two excluded boundary points. Its enclosed area is 16π ≈ 50.265, which rounds to 50.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-circular-locus","label":"Use the centroid's median ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-circular-locus":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p188","cnm_p192"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p188","cnm_p192"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 200","reason":"The square formed by the four side midpoints has both diagonals of length 30. Scaling it by 2/3 about P gives the centroid quadrilateral, whose perpendicular diagonals both have length 20. Its area is 20·20/2=200.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"],"id":"centroid-scaling","label":"Scale the side-midpoint quadrilateral","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-scaling":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_p150","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"],"id":"solution:centroid-coordinate-averages:46692e54eeb1","label":"Let the unknown coordinates cancel","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-coordinate-averages":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_084","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_084","cnm_p150","cnm_082","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 44, with cos C = 5√13/26","reason":"Scale AE to 1 and take A = (0,0), E = (1,0), and G = (1/2,√3/2). Then C = (2,0) and the centroid relation gives B = (-1/2,3√3/2). Thus BC = √13 and cos C = (5/2)/√13 = 5√13/26, giving 5 + 26 + 13 = 44.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-triangle-acd","label":"Apply the cosine law in triangle ACD","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-triangle-acd":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_171"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"drop-a-perpendicular","label":"Use a right-triangle projection","methodDirectNodeIds":{"drop-a-perpendicular":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_276","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_276","cnm_171"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinates-and-dot-product","label":"Place the equilateral triangle on an axis","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-and-dot-product":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_h086","cnm_h088","cnm_h090","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_h086","cnm_h088","cnm_h090","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 1/12","reason":"E is the midpoint of AB and N the midpoint of AE, so EN=AB/4. M has height h/3 above AB; therefore [MNE]/[ABC]=(1/4)(1/3)=1/12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055"],"id":"centroid-area-comparisons","label":"Use the centroid ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-area-comparisons":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148","cnm_113","cnm_055"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055"],"id":"median-equal-area-partitions","label":"Use only