pe_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_p150"],"id":"perpendicular-medians","label":"Perpendicular medians and triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"perpendicular-medians":["cnm_177","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_p150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (108)","reason":"By central symmetry, take centroid G=(1,1), with circumradius R. At each triangle vertex, xy=1 gives R²=(x+y)²-2(x+y). Thus the three sums x+y take two quadratic-root values s,t, with one occurring twice, since a fixed sum allows at most two points on xy=1. Hence 2s+t=6 and s+t=2, giving s=4 and R²=8. The side squared is 3R²=24, so the area squared is 3·24²/16=108.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"hyperbola-symmetry-and-centroid","label":"Use symmetry and the centroid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hyperbola-symmetry-and-centroid":["cnm_238","cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_133","cnm_h173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_238","cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_133","cnm_h173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 12+10√3","reason":"The centroid condition gives AB=AD=BD. For θ=∠BCD, the cosine rule gives BD²=40−24cosθ, so the total area is K=10√3+6(sinθ−√3cosθ)=10√3+12sin(θ−60°)≤10√3+12. Equality at θ=150° is attainable with a convex quadrilateral.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroids-and-shifted-sine","label":"Centroids and a sine maximum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroids-and-shifted-sine":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_p150","math:general-cosine-law-application","math:triangle-sine-area","cnm_h070","cnm_h066"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_p150","math:general-cosine-law-application","math:triangle-sine-area","cnm_h070","cnm_h066"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"coordinates-and-cauchy","label":"Coordinates and a square bound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinates-and-cauchy":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","cnm_139","cnm_h173","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","math:cauchy-schwarz","cnm_140"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","cnm_139","cnm_h173","cnm_p148","cnm_p150","math:cauchy-schwarz","cnm_140"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 96","reason":"Let P be the medians' intersection, the centroid. Then PM=PC=(2/3)*12=8. Because the medians are perpendicular, area(MPC)=8*8/2=32. This is one third of area(AMC), so the requested area is 96.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-area-ratios","label":"Centroid lengths and areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-area-ratios":["math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_064"],"id":"centroid-base-height","label":"Use the centroid to find a height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-base-height":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148","cnm_113","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 75 square inches","reason":"AD and EB are medians of triangle ACE. Their centroid F has height 30/3=10 above CE. Since DE=15, area DFE=15·10/2=75.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-height","label":"Median and centroid ratios","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-height":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2/3","reason":"AN and CM are medians of triangle ABC, so O is its centroid and [AOC]=[ABC]/3. Consequently [AOCD]/[ABCD]=1/2+(1/2)/3=2/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-area-division","label":"Centroid and triangle areas","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-area-division":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (1/16)","reason":"After half a perimeter, the particles swap positions and the midpoint locus repeats. Its three vertices are the midpoints of the medians of ABC. Relative to the centroid, each is 1/4 of the corresponding vertex vector. Thus the enclosed triangle has area ratio (1/4)²=1/16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"centroid-homothety","label":"Scale about the centroid","nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p111"],"id":"solution:centroid-homothety:8aa73fe02fd6","label":"Find the scale factor along the medians","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-homothety":["cnm_272","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_272","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_275","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_p111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, sqrt(17)","reason":"Put the centroid at the origin, A=(u,0), B=(0,v), and C=(-u,-v). Then BC^2=u^2+4v^2=49 and AC^2=4u^2+v^2=36. Adding gives u^2+v^2=17, which equals AB^2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q36","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"centroid-right-triangles","label":"Use centroid ratios and right triangles","nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174","cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-coordinate-axes","label":"Use the perpendicular medians as coordinate axes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-coordinate-axes":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h173","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h173","cnm_090"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_075"],"id":"solution:centroid-right-triangles:8f00a4839a74","label":"Centroid ratios and right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-right-triangles":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, a circle if a>s²","reason":"If G is the centroid, then PA²+PB²+PC²=3PG²+s². Hence PG²=(a-s²)/3, which defines a positive-radius circle exactly when a>s². At equality the locus is the single point G.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-vector-expansion","label":"Expand about the centroid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-vector-expansion":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h081","cnm_h089"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h081","cnm_h089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: s√6/2","reason":"Let G be the intersection of the medians. Then BG=(2/3)BN. Right triangles BGC and BCN are similar, since they share the angle at B, so BC²=BG·BN=(2/3)BN². With BC=s, this gives BN=s√6/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-and-similar-triangles","label":"Centroid ratios and right-triangle similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-and-similar-triangles":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_268","cnm_080"],"id":"centroid-and-right-triangle-similarity","label":"Centroid ratio and similar right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-and-right-triangle-similarity":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_268","cnm_080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Native reconciliation under the recorded task assumptions; no blind-review claim.","derivedAnswer":"B, 3+sqrt(69)/3","reason":"The three lower centers lie at height 1 and form an equilateral triangle of side 2. Their common circumcenter is at horizontal distance 2/sqrt(3) from each. The large center must be above it with center distance 3 to each lower center, so its vertical rise is sqrt(9-4/3)=sqrt(69)/3. Its top is another radius 2 higher, giving 3+sqrt(69)/3.","recordedAnswer":"B","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["larger_sphere_center_above_lower_centers"],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"synthetic-centers","label":"Use the equilateral center triangle and a vertical right triangle","nodeIds":["cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h106","cnm_113","cnm_265"],"id":"solution:synthetic-centers:e9bd5ebb6f13","label":"Use the triangle of sphere centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"synthetic-centers":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_139","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_174","cnm_h106","cnm_113","cnm_265"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, πp²/27","reason":"The side is p/3. Since p/3=2R sin60°=√3R, R²=p²/27 and the circle area is πp²/27.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"altitude-and-center","label":"Equilateral altitude and center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-and-center":["cnm_137","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 1.5","reason":"Let the altitude be h and the centroid be G. The two base vertices and G form a right isosceles triangle with hypotenuse √2, so G is √2/2 above the base. Thus h=3√2/2 and the area is (√2)(3√2/2)/2=3/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-right-triangle","label":"Use the centroid","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-right-triangle":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137","cnm_174"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055"],"id":"centroid-and-right-triangle","label":"Use the centroid and a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-and-right-triangle":["cnm_113","cnm_p148","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137","cnm_187"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_p148","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137","cnm_187","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 3sqrt(6) inches","reason":"At centroid G, AG=2, BG=4, and area ABG=sqrt(15). Taking G=(0,0), A=(2,0) gives B=(±1,sqrt(15)). Since C=-A-B, the third median squared is 36 or 54. The value 6 makes AB=AC, so unequal sides require 3sqrt(6).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_174"],"id":"centroid-coordinates","label":"Use the centroid and coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-coordinates":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_084"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_084","cnm_113","cnm_p148","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 2+2√6","reason":"Use outer vertices (t,t,t),(t,-t,-t),(-t,t,-t),(-t,-t,t), and ball centers with the same sign patterns at scale u. Mutual tangency gives 2√2u=2. Tangency to adjacent faces gives (t-u)/√3=1. Hence s=2√2t=2+2√6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Uses the standard circumscribed packing in which each tetrahedron face is tangent to its three adjacent balls.","Uses the standard symmetric circumscription in which each outer face is tangent to the three balls adjacent to that face."],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_075"],"id":"regular-tetrahedron-offsets","label":"Use distances from the common center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"regular-tetrahedron-offsets":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 2, with m = 12 or 3/4","reason":"For signed leg lengths a and b, the median slopes are -2b/a and -b/(2a), whose ratio is 4. Thus the other slope is 12 or 3/4. Both are realizable with the specified intercepts and nonzero legs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1986_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"use-centroid-coordinate-vectors","label":"Centroid coordinate relations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-centroid-coordinate-vectors":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h087","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:centroid-ratios","cnm_h087","cnm_090"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: (3+2√3)/3","reason":"The equilateral triangle of centers has side 2 and altitude √3. Its circumradius is two-thirds of that altitude, or 2√3/3. Internal tangency therefore gives the large radius as 1+2√3/3 = (3+2√3)/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equilateral-altitude-and-centroid","label":"Altitude and centroid ratio","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equilateral-altitude-and-centroid":["cnm_137","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 5:4","reason":"Triangles AEF and CBF are similar with side ratio AE:CB = 1:2, giving AF:FC = 1:2. If the parallelogram has area S, then [CFB] = S/3 and [AEF] = S/12. Hence [CDEF] = S/2-S/12 = 5S/12, and the required ratio is 5:4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"centroid-of-abd","label":"Use the centroid of triangle ABD","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-of-abd":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_113","math:centroid-ratios"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, s²/6","reason":"The triangle's inradius is r=s√3/6. The square's diagonal is 2r, so its area is (2r)²/2=2r²=s²/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q03","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equilateral-altitude-centroid","label":"Find the inradius from the altitude","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equilateral-altitude-centroid":["cnm_139","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_191"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_191"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (1/3)","reason":"For tetrahedron edge length s, the face altitudes give CM=DM=sqrt(3)s/2, while CD=s. Therefore cos(CMD)=(CM^2+DM^2-CD^2)/(2CM·DM)=1/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"project-to-face-center","label":"Project onto the opposite face","methodDirectNodeIds":{"project-to-face-center":["cnm_174","cnm_265","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_276","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_265","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_276","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 9","reason":"The base circumradius is 2sqrt(3), so h=sqrt(15-12)=sqrt(3). The base area is 9sqrt(3), giving V=(1/3)*9sqrt(3)*sqrt(3)=9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1973_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_137","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"],"id":"circumcenter-height-volume","label":"Find the perpendicular height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circumcenter-height-volume":["cnm_h111","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_265","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h111","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_265","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1/4","reason":"Let the outer altitude be h. Its center is h/3 above BC, so the center is h/3 - h/6 = h/6 above B'C'. The inner altitude is therefore 3(h/6) = h/2. The area ratio is (1/2)^2 = 1/4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_112","cnm_139","cnm_271"],"id":"centroid-altitudes","label":"Use the center's position on an altitude","methodDirectNodeIds":{"centroid-altitudes":["cnm_272","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_272","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_137","cnm_113","cnm_112","cnm_139","cnm_271"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (2+√3)/3","reason":"Normalize AB=1. Each center is √3/6 outside its base, so EG=1+√3/3. Therefore [EFGH]/[ABCD]=EG²/2=(2+√3)/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_174","cnm_113","cnm_p089"],"id":"center-offset-and-diagonal","label":"Find the center offsets and a diagonal","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-offset-and-diagonal":["cnm_266","cnm_191","math:centroid-ratios"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_191","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_139","cnm_174","cnm_113","cnm_p089"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 552","reason":"For the shown orientation, adjacent centers have separation 8, with components 6 and s−2√3 relative to a triangle side of length s. Thus (s−2√3)²+36=64, giving s=2(√3+√7). The area is (√3/4)s²=10√3+6√7=√300+√252, so a+b=552.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The shown large tangent configuration is the branch with area10sqrt(3)+6sqrt(7); the other positive branch has area10sqrt(3)-6sqrt(7)<2 and cannot satisfy the given sum of two positive integer square roots."],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-center-cosine-law","label":"Common center and cosine law","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-center-cosine-law":["cnm_245","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_h091","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_245","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_h091","cnm_221"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5/27 closest to 0.2","reason":"Regular tetrahedron vertices (+,+,+),(+,-,-),(-,+,-),(-,-,+) in coordinates +/-1 give R=sqrt(3), face distance r=1/sqrt(3). An exterior face-tangent sphere center is (r+rho)n. The furthest point from O has radius r+2rho; internal contact with the circum-ball gives r+2rho=R, so rho=r. Face normals have pairwise dot product -1/3; their centers 2r*n have pairwise distances 2r*sqrt(8/3)>2r. All four are internally tangent to the big sphere and externally tangent to the central sphere, so disjoint interiors give uniform volume ratio 5*(r/R)^3=5/27. Internal tangency is derived by the radial maximum, not imported from an external-tangency-only node.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Interprets the randomly selected point as uniform with respect to volume.","Interprets selection at random as uniform with respect to volume."],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_271","cnm_h106","cnm_285"],"id":"tetrahedron-altitudes-and-volumes","label":"Altitudes, pyramid volumes, and similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tetrahedron-altitudes-and-volumes":["math:geometric-probability","math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_h112","cnm_h111","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["math:geometric-probability","math:sphere-tangency-distances","cnm_h112","cnm_h111","cnm_174","math:centroid-ratios","cnm_272","cnm_139","cnm_271","cnm_h106","cnm_285"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:centroid-ratios","status":"mapped","total":37}
